 ## Overview
-Hierarchical Grid Organization with Macrocells (HUG) is a sparse acceleration structure designed to handle large variance in object sizes within a scene, where standard uniform grids fail because the average object size is not a suitable cell dimension [S2, unofficial]. It solves this by grouping primitives by size and location to construct local grids, which are then recursively inserted into a global grid [S1, unofficial]. Unlike recursive grids where voxels cannot overlap, HUG uses "mailboxes" during traversal because nested grids are treated as objects themselves [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Grid Resolution Selection via Lambda](3270-grid-resolution-selection-via-lambda.md) to determine the base resolution of the finest grid level.
+Hierarchical Grid Organization with Macrocells (HUG) is a sparse acceleration structure designed to handle large variance in object sizes within a scene, where standard uniform grids fail because the average object size is not a suitable cell dimension [S2, unofficial]. It solves this by grouping primitives by size and location to construct local grids, which are then recursively inserted into a global grid [S1, unofficial]. Unlike recursive grids where voxels cannot overlap, HUG uses "mailboxes" during traversal because nested grids are treated as objects themselves [S1, unofficial]. It determines the base resolution of the finest grid level using a scaling factor $\lambda$ [S3, S4], building on [Grid Resolution Selection via Lambda](3270-grid-resolution-selection-via-lambda.md).
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A set of primitives (particles/objects), the scene bounding box volume $V$, and the scene diagonal $d$.
-**Outputs:** A hierarchical grid structure consisting of a global grid and nested local grids, ready for ray traversal.
